WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:09.520
To say Elon Musk is a busy guy is an&nbsp;
understatement between running SpaceX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:16.720
Tesla, and Neuralink, he apparently plans his day&nbsp;
in five-minute slots. Time is precious. So one day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.480
in 2016 while sitting in another LA traffic&nbsp;
jam, he had enough and tweeted he was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.760
to build a tunnel boring machine and just start&nbsp;
digging. In case anyone thought he was kidding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.880
he followed up with: I'm actually going to do&nbsp;
this. It wasn't a spur of the moment decision to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.960
found The Boring Company. Musk had been thinking&nbsp;
about it for a while. Traffic is soul-destroying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.000
It's like acid on the soul, it's horrible. And&nbsp;
his solution might just end traffic for good

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.520
When Boring built its test tunnel leaving the&nbsp;
parking lot of SpaceX's headquarters in Hawthorne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.480
California, the aim was to find a better&nbsp;
way for private vehicles to get around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.960
But the plans have now expanded into a&nbsp;
public transportation system called the Loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.240
where those who don't own cars can&nbsp;
hop on a vehicle cycling through.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.760
The first Loop will be in Las Vegas built for&nbsp;
the massive convention center which hosts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:22.080
Consumer Electronics Show every January. A walk&nbsp;
from one end to another normally takes 15 minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.120
That's now turned into a one-minute ride.&nbsp;
Visitors will be packed into a Model X,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.440
Model 3, or a modified Tesla that can fit up&nbsp;
to 16 people. This is an earlier prototype.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.120
The vehicles will be zipping through the&nbsp;
tunnels at up to 155 mph, initially with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.720
driver and eventually autonomously. An electric&nbsp;
skate was supposed to help center the car. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.600
was axed in favor of retractable wheels but&nbsp;
both ideas have been abandoned and instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.200
the vehicle will rely on its own tires driving&nbsp;
through a paved tunnel. The convention center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.920
Loop could be the beginning of a bigger Vegas&nbsp;
network stretching all the way down the Strip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:05.440
potentially one day connecting to Los Angeles if&nbsp;
the website is any indication. In LA, there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.800
proposal to take fans to and from Dodger Stadium&nbsp;
so instead of sitting in traffic for an hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:16.160
the Dugout Loop as it's called would only be a&nbsp;
four-minute ride and cost a dollar each way. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:23.120
mean wouldn't it be incredible if you could travel&nbsp;
around LA, New York, DC, Chicago, Paris, London,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:29.840
anywhere at 150 miles an hour? Expanding to major&nbsp;
cities around the world will only be possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.520
by bringing down costs - the same strategy must&nbsp;
apply to the rocket business. The final liftoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:41.760
of Atlantis. Space shuttle launches used to&nbsp;
be really expensive - $450 million per mission

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:50.160
Then SpaceX came along and dropped it down to&nbsp;
$62 million for a Falcon 9 flight largely because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:56.080
it's able to re-use parts of the rocket. In the&nbsp;
same way, building tunnels is very expensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.440
Los Angeles' purple line subway extension has a&nbsp;
price tag of more than a billion dollars a mile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.960
yet the one-mile demo tunnel only costs $10&nbsp;
million because The Boring Company applied the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:13.440
principle of reusability responsible for SpaceX's&nbsp;
success. Typically, the excavated dirt is thrown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.720
away and the process of taking it off-site is very&nbsp;
costly. Instead, the dirt will be used to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:25.520
build the tunnel's reinforcing segments on-site&nbsp;
and any leftovers will be turned into bricks to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:31.440
sold, paying for the dirt removal itself or given&nbsp;
away for free for affordable housing projects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.960
Another way to cut costs is by building smaller&nbsp;
tunnels. The standard size for a single lane is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.320
28 feet in diameter. Boring's will only be&nbsp;
12 feet which doesn't leave a lot of room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.880
so although technically any electric vehicle&nbsp;
with autonomous capabilities will be able to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:53.200
them in the future, they'll have to fit. Smaller&nbsp;
tunnels also make building faster. Tunneling today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:59.600
is slower than a snail. This is our goal: we must&nbsp;
be faster than a snail. They plan on beating Gary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.960
the company's pet snail by bringing a different&nbsp;
approach to drilling. Usually, a tunnel boring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:11.600
machine or TBM drill, then stops, as the segments&nbsp;
are installed. The Boring Company wants to do both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:18.320
at the same time and with a more powerful machine.&nbsp;
A standard TBM can dig one mile in eight to 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.240
weeks whereas Boring's third-generation TBM, the&nbsp;
custom-designed Prufrock, is meant to go more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:30.480
a mile a week. And if it moves that quickly, the&nbsp;
system could rival subways or in Musk's words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.600
"crush" subways. While a train has to stop at&nbsp;
every station, Loop passengers would travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.400
directly to their destination, skipping all the&nbsp;
other stops. And unlike a subway, there isn't a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.880
limit to the number of stations. They can be&nbsp;
as discreet as a couple of parking spaces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.720
It would also be as much as nine times the speed&nbsp;
of a train. If you find it uncomfortable it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.320
be hard to handle what comes next because the&nbsp;
Loop is a stepping stone toward the Hyperloop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.160
The Hyperloop is meant for city-to-city&nbsp;
transport at more than 600 miles an hour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.360
We're talking airplane speeds. So passengers&nbsp;
and cargo will be in a pressurized pod. It could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.720
either connect to the Loop or fit within the Loop&nbsp;
tunnels. For the past few years, SpaceX has held a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:22.800
competition for students and non-students to build&nbsp;
sub-scale prototypes. Although this technology may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.000
captivate minds, there are questions about&nbsp;
how safe a Loop or Hyperloop would be. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.160
proposed 35-mile DC to Baltimore route has been&nbsp;
criticized for not having enough emergency exits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.880
The plan is to construct up to 70 ventilation&nbsp;
shafts serving as exits that would be spaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:45.440
as far as two miles apart. Boring notes the Loop&nbsp;
is designed to be the fastest and safest system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:51.280
ever and says it will comply with safety and fire&nbsp;
protection standards. Still, some engineers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:57.520
skeptical this will succeed on a large scale.&nbsp;
But the same was said of SpaceX when it began.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.640
And many thought Tesla would never learn the art&nbsp;
of mass production. If Musk is known for anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:08.880
it's beating the odds with engineering feats.&nbsp;
Finally, finally, finally there's something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.080
Something that I think could&nbsp;
solve the goddamn traffic problem.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.880
The future should be anything but boring.&nbsp;
Hey guys, I appreciate you watching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

